  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Picks up FluentImporter 6.2.0.\n\nFluentImporter 6.2.0 reorders the generated import-template legend to lead with the allowed-value tables instead of\nthe column reference table, and stops appending a dropdown label that merely repeats its value, so a value set whose\nvalues are already words renders \"Yes\" rather than \"Yes - Yes\". Files generated by either version import identically.\n\nPrevious release notes follow.\n\n2.4.5 — Picks up FluentImporter 6.1.0 and FileExporter 8.1.0.\n\nFluentImporter 6.1.0 adds WithExampleText, which declares the exact text a generated sample cell should carry. It is\nwhat lets a column converted from text into a collection be marked required: an example typed to the destination\nproperty was rendered as its type name, so the generated template failed its own parse on the blank cell.\n\nFileExporter 8.1.0 writes booleans as the text Yes/No instead of as logical cells, which used to render in the\nviewer's own Excel UI language and disagreed with CSV, and lets a declared HasFormat win over the property's CLR\ntype, which had made HasFormat(Date) dead on a DateTime and HasFormat(Integer) dead on a decimal. No public API break\nin either.\n\nPrevious release notes follow.\n\n2.4.4 — Picks up FileExporter 8.0.0, which adds ExportOptions for per-request overrides — localized column headers, an enum label resolver, and explicit file and sheet names — none of which were reachable before, because an ExportRule is a boot-time singleton. It also stamps the file name's timestamp at export time instead of at rule construction, raises the name cap from 30 to 100 characters, substitutes WithName's {DateTime} placeholder instead of shipping it as a literal, rejects an undefined export format explicitly, stops rendering \"7 - \" for undefined enum values, and makes CSV percentages agree with XLSX. No public API break.",
